\n   \\[\n   2k\\ge 2n-2\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;k\\ge n-1 .\n   \\]\n   But if \\(k=n-1\\) we would have exactly one vertical line and \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Those \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would already be needed to cover the \\(2n-2\\) points of the first two columns, leaving **no sunny line** left to cover any point in column \\(x=n\\) (the point \\((n,1)\\) must be covered).  \n   Therefore a configuration with \\(k=n-1\\) cannot exist, and consequently  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma 1 gives \\(k=0\\); Lemma 2 supplies a construction for every \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\); Lemma 3 shows that \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible.  \n   Hence the admissible values are exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge 1}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), undefined (vertical), and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Base configuration, \\(k=0\\))\n\nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n)\n\\]\n\nare pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\supseteq T_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); thus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_a\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Construction for \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\))\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nDefine  \n\n* vertical lines \\(V_i:\\;x=i\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-k\\);\n* sunny lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n\\[\n\\ell_j:\\;y = x + c_j,\\qquad c_j:=-n+j\\quad(j=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nThen the \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{L\\in\\{V_i\\}\\cup\\{\\ell_j\\}}L\\supseteq T_n .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \n\n1. **Distinctness and sunny count.**  \n   The constants \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_k\\) are pairwise different,