e a sunny line can cover **at most two** points of \\(S\\).\n\nIf \\(v=1\\) (the only possible case when \\(k\\ge n-1\\)), the single vertical line can cover at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S\\); the remaining \\(|S|-n = n-1\\) points must be covered by sunny lines, each contributing at most two points. Hence we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\frac{n-1}{2}\\right\\rceil\n\\]\n\nsunny lines. This inequality is always satisfied, so we must look deeper.\n\nObserve that the point \\((n,1)\\) (the unique point of the last column) also belongs to \\(T_n\\).  \nA vertical line cannot cover it, and a sunny line of slope \\(1\\) passes through \\((n,1)\\) only if its intercept equals \\(-n+1\\).  \nIf \\(k=n-1\\), the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines are already forced (by the need to cover the \\(2n-2\\) points of the first two columns) to have intercepts \\(-n+2,\\dots , -1\\) (the only \\(n-1\\) distinct values that allow coverage of those columns, as shown in Lemma 2).  \nNone of these intercepts equals \\(-n+1\\); consequently none of the sunny lines passes through \\((n,1)\\).  \nThus \\((n,1)\\) would remain uncovered – a contradiction.\n\nHence a configuration with \\(k\\ge n-1\\) cannot exist, i.e. \\(k\\le n-2\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Theorem (Complete classification)\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLemma 1 provides a configuration for \\(k=0\\).  \nLemma 2 constructs, for each \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\), a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \nLemma 3 shows that \\(k\\ge n-1\\) is impossible.  \nTherefore the admissible values are precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Verification  \n\n| \\(n\\) | admissible \\(k\\) | example for \\(k=n-2\\) (using Lemma 2) |\n|------|------------------|-----