those points satisfy \\(a+b=n+1\\) and \\(a'+b'=n+1\\). Subtracting gives \\((a-a')+(b-b')=0\\), i.e. the vector between the two points is \\((t,-t)\\) for some non‑zero \\(t\\). Hence the line has slope \\(-1\\), which is forbidden for a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\nConsequently each sunny line contributes at most one point to each of the three sets.  \n\nThe total numbers of points in the three sets are  \n\n\\[\n|A_n|=n,\\qquad |B_n|=n,\\qquad |C_n|=n-2\\;(n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\nThus the total number of points that can be covered **exclusively** by sunny lines is at most  \n\n\\[\n3k\\;(\\text{one from each set per sunny line}).\n\\]\n\nAll remaining points must be covered by the non‑sunny lines.  \nObserve that the two points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) lie on the same horizontal line \\(y=1\\); similarly \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,n)\\) lie on the same vertical line \\(x=1\\). Hence a single non‑sunny line can cover **two** points of the union \\(A_n\\cup B_n\\). No non‑sunny line can cover more than two points of this union because any line parallel to one of the forbidden directions meets each of the two axes in at most one point.\n\nTherefore, after using all \\(k\\) sunny lines, at least  \n\n\\[\n|A_n|+|B_n|+|C_n|-3k = (2n+n-2)-3k = 3n-2-3k\n\\]\n\npoints remain uncovered. Each non‑sunny line can cover at most two of them, so we need at least  \n\n\\[\n\\left\\lceil\\frac{3n-2-3k}{2}\\right\\rceil\n\\]\n\nnon‑sunny lines. Since the total number of lines is \\(n\\),\n\n\\[\nn-k \\;\\ge\\; \\left\\lceil\\frac{3n-2-3k}{2}\\right\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nA short elementary manipulation (checking the two possible parities of \\(3n-2-3k\\)) yields the equivalent inequality  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3,4\\) the same argument gives the sharper bound \\(k\\le1\\) (because \\(C_3\\) and \\(C_4\\) contain respectively \\(1\\) and \\(2\\) points, and a direct inspection shows that two sunny lines would already force a repetition of a line). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nWe now show that for each integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le \n\\begin{cases}\n1, & n=3,4,\\\\\nn-2, & n\\ge5,\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1  The two always‑present non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_V:\\;x=1\\qquad\\text{(vertical, non‑sunny)},\\qquad\nL_H:\\;y=1\\qquad\\text{(horizontal, non‑sunny)}.\n\\]\n\nThese two lines already cover all points of \\(A_n\\cup B_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 3.2  Sunny lines covering points of \\(C_n\\)\n\nList the points of \\(C_n\\) as  \n\n\\[\nC_n=\\{(2,n-1),(3,n-2),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose any \\(k\\) of them, say the first \\(k\\) points \\((2,n-1),\\dots ,(k+1,n-k)\\).  \nFor each chosen point \\((a,b)\\) draw a line \\(L_{a,b}\\) with slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) that passes through \\((a,b)\\) and **does not** pass through any other point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\). This is always possible because the set of forbidden slopes is finite; we may, for instance, take the line  \n\n\\[\nL_{a,b}:\\; y = \\bigl(\\tfrac{1}{2}\\bigr)(x-a)+b,\n\\]\n\nwhose slope \\(1/2\\) is sunny and which meets \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) only at \\((a,b)\\) (the other points of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) lie either on the left of \\(x=a\\) or above the line). The \\(k\\) lines obtained in this way are distinct and sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.3  Remaining non‑sunny lines  \n\nAfter step 3.2, the points of \\(C_n\\) that are still uncovered are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\{(k+2,n-k-1),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\},\n\\]\n\na set of size \\((n-2)-k\\).  \nFor each such point \\((a,b)\\) we add a line parallel to one of the three forbidden directions, for instance the line  \n\n\\[\ny = -x + (a+b)\n\\]\n\nwhich has slope \\(-1\\) (hence non‑sunny) and passes through \\((a,b)\\). Because distinct points of \\(C_n\\) have distinct sums \\(a+b\\), these lines are all different.  \n\nThus we have added 