            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n### 1.  Notation and decomposition of the required points  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nWrite  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA_n&=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\\\\nB_n&=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\\\\nC_n&=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge2,\\;b\\ge2,\\;a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nClearly \\(\\mathcal P_n=A_n\\cup B_n\\cup C_n\\) and the three sets are pairwise disjoint.\nTheir cardinalities are  \n\n\\[\n|A_n|=|B_n|=n,\\qquad |C_n|=n-2\\;(n\\ge3).\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nk:=|\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L_i\\text{ is sunny}\\}|\n\\]\n\nbe the number of sunny lines.\n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nA sunny line contains at most one point of each of the sets \\(A_n,B_n,C_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points of \\(A_n\\) they would both have abscissa \\(1\\); the line would be vertical, contradicting sunniness.  \nThe same argument with \\(B_n\\) shows that two points of \\(B_n\\) would force the line to be horizontal.  \nIf a line contained two distinct points \\((a,b),(a',b')\\in C_n\\) then \\(a+b=a'+b'=n+1\\); subtracting gives \\((a-a')+(b-b')=0\\), i.e. the direction vector is \\((t,-t)\\) for some non‑zero \\(t\\). Hence the line has slope \\(-1\\), again impossible for a sunny line. ∎\n\n\n\nConsequently a sunny line can contribute at most three points to the covering of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) (one from each of \\(A_n,B_n,C_n\\)).  \nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(3k\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nAll remaining points, i.e.  \n\n\\[\n|A_n|+|B_n|+|C_n|-3k = (2n+n-2)-3k = 3n-2-3k,\n\\]\n\nmust be covered by the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nA non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three forbidden directions; such a line meets each of the two axes in at most one point, so it can contain **at most two** points of \\(A_n\\cup B_n\\).  \nHence at least  \n\n\\[\n\\Big\\lceil\\frac{3n-2-3k}{2}\\Big\\rceil\n\\]\n\nnon‑sunny lines are necessary. Because we have exactly \\(n-k\\) of them, we must have  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge\\Big\\lceil\\frac{3n-2-3k}{2}\\Big\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nA routine case‑by‑case check of the parity of \\(3n-2-3k\\) yields the equivalent inequality  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFor \\(n=3,4\\) the same argument gives the sharper bound \\(k\\le1\\) (the set \\(C_n\\) contains only one or two points, and two sunny lines would already force a repetition of a line). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) satisfying  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n1, & n=3,4,\\\\\nn-2, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe shall exhibit \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.1  Two permanent non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_V:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_H:\\;y=1 .\n\\]\n\nThese two lines cover all points of \\(A_n\\cup B_n\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Sunny lines covering \\(k\\) points of \\(C_n\\)\n\nList the points of \\(C_n\\) as  \n\n\\[\nC_n=\\{(2,n-1),(3,n-2),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose any \\(k\\) of them, for definiteness the first \\(k\\) points  \n\\[\nP_i=(i+1,\\;n-i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(P_i\\) draw the line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-\\bigl(n-i\\bigr)=\\frac12\\bigl(x-(i+1)\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(S_i\\) is sunny.  \nBecause the chosen points are distinct and the slope is the same for all \\(S_i\\), the lines are distinct.  \nEach \\(S_i\\) meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) only at its defining point \\(P_i\\) (all other points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lie either to the left of \\(x=i+1\\) or above the line).  \n\nThus we have exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering one point of \\(C_n\\).\n\n#### 3.3  Covering the remaining points of \\(C_n\\)\n\nThe set of uncovered points of \\(C_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\nC_n\\setminus\\{P_1,\\dots ,P_k\\}\n   =\\{(k+2,n-k-1),\\dots ,(n-1,2)\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich contains \\(n-2-k\\) points.  \nFor each such point \\((a,b)\\) we add a **non‑sunny** line as follows:\n\n* if the vertical line \\(x=a\\) has not been used yet, take \\(L_{a}= \\{x=a\\}\\);\n* otherwise take the horizontal line \\(y=b\\).\n\nBecause the abscissae \\(a\\) (and the ordinates \\(b\\)) are all distinct among the points of \\(C_n\\), this rule yields \\(n-2-k\\) distinct lines, none of which coincides with \\(L_V\\) (which is \\(x=1\\)) or \\(L_H\\) (which is \\(y=1\\)).  \nAll these lines are parallel to a forbidden direction, hence non‑sunny, and each contains exactly the point for which it was created.\n\n#### 3.4  Counting  \n\nWe have assembled  \n\n* the two permanent lines \\(L_V,L_H\\);\n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\);\n* the \\(n-2-k\\) extra non‑sunny lines described in 3.3.\n\nAll together they are \\(2+k+(n-2-k)=n\\) distinct lines.  \nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of them:\n\n* points of \\(A_n\\cup B_n\\) are on \\(L_V\\) or \\(L_H\\);\n* each point of \\(C_n\\) is either one of the \\(k\\) chosen points (covered by a sunny line) or one of the remaining points (covered by the vertical/horizontal line assigned to it).\n\nThus the construction satisfies the required covering condition and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nFor \\(n=3,4\\) the same procedure works, the only difference being that the bound forces \\(k\\le1\\); the construction with \\(k=0\\) or \\(k=1\\) is obtained by taking the appropriate number of sunny lines among the (at most one) points of \\(C_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe upper bound proved in §2 shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines (and for \\(n=3,4\\) not more than one).  \nThe explicit constructions of §3 demonstrate that every integer \\(k\\) within these limits is indeed attainable.  \n\nHence the set of possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3\\text{ or }n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
