ailed Solution**\n\n### 1.  The set of required points  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right‑\nangled triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) let  \n\n\\[\nC_i=\\{(i,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n+1-i\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe columns \\(C_1,\\dots ,C_n\\) are pairwise disjoint and  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}C_i .\n\\]\n\nTheir cardinalities are \\(|C_i|=n+1-i\\); in particular \\(|C_1|=n\\) and\n\\(|C_n|=1\\).\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal),\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) be any family of \\(n\\) distinct lines\ncovering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) and let  \n\n\\[\nk:=|\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L_i\\text{ is sunny}\\}|\n\\]\n\nbe the number of sunny lines.\n\n#### Lemma 2.1  \nA sunny line meets each column \\(C_i\\) in at most one point.\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf a line contained two distinct points of the same column, those points would\nhave the same abscissa, forcing the line to be vertical, contradicting the\ndefinition of sunny. ∎\n\nConsequently a sunny line can cover at most one point of each column, i.e. at\nmost \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\nConsider column \\(C_1\\), which contains \\(n\\) points. By Lemma 2.1 a sunny\nline can meet \\(C_1\\) in at most one point, so at least \\(n-1\\) points of\n\\(C_1\\) must be covered by non‑sunny lines. Since a non‑sunny line can be\nvertical, the only way to cover all points of \\(C_1\\) with the fewest lines is\nto keep the vertical line \\(x=1\\) as a non‑sunny line. Thus at least one\nnon‑sunny line is forced, and the total number of non‑sunny lines is at least\n\\(n-1\\).\n\nIf a column \\(C_i\\) contains at least two points (\\(i\\le n-1\\)), the same\nargument shows that it must remain a vertical line; otherwise the points of\nthat column other than the (possible) diagonal point would be uncovered. The\nonly columns that consist of a single point are \\(C_n\\) (point \\((n,1)\\)) and,\nwhen \\(n\\ge5\\), also \\(C_{n-1}\\) (point \\((n-1,2)\\)). Hence at most these two\ncolumns can be replaced by sunny lines.\n\nTherefore  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n1, & n=3,4,\\\\[2mm]\nn-2, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Construction attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (1).\n\n#### 3.1  The base family of vertical lines  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) take the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n\\) lines are distinct, non‑sunny, and together they cover every\ncolumn \\(C_i\\); hence they cover the whole set \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Replacing columns by sunny lines  \n\nSelect any \\(k\\) columns among those that contain exactly one point:\n\\(C_n\\) and, when \\(n\\ge5\\), also \\(C_{n-1}\\).  \nFor each selected column \\(C_i\\) (so \\(i=n\\) or \\(i=n-1\\)) delete the vertical\nline \\(L_i\\) and insert the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\frac12\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\frac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus \\(S_i\\) is sunny.\nBecause the line has a non‑vertical direction, it meets the integer lattice\ninside the triangle only at its defining point \\((i,n+1-i)\\).\n\nAfter performing this replacement for the chosen \\(k\\) columns we have:\n\n* exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (the \\(S_i\\)’s);\n* the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines are the untouched vertical lines \\(L_i\\) for the\n  other columns;\n* the total number of lines is still \\(n\\).\n\n#### 3.3  Verification of coverage  \n\n*If a column was not replaced*, its v