+\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nUsing (5) for \\(|\\mathcal P_n|\\) and simplifying (7) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\le kn+\\frac{(n-k)(n+k+1)}{2}\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\nk\\le n-2 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThus no configuration can contain more than \\(n-2\\) sunny lines.\nFor \\(n=3,4\\) the right‑hand side of (8) equals \\(1\\); for \\(n\\ge5\\) it equals\n\\(n-2\\).  Hence the maximal possible number of sunny lines is exactly the\nquantity stated in the answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  Constructions attaining every admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge3\\) and an integer \\(k\\) satisfying (8).\n\n#### 3.1  Vertical backbone  \n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\) take the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i .\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n\\) lines are distinct, non‑sunny, and together they cover every\ncolumn \\(C_i\\), hence all of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Replacing columns that consist of a single point  \n\nThe columns that contain exactly one point are  \n\n\\[\nC_n=\\{(n,1)\\},\n\\qquad\n\\text{and, when }n\\ge5,\\;\nC_{n-1}=\\{(n-1,2)\\}.\n\\]\n\nChoose any \\(k\\) of these columns (possible precisely because of (8)).\nFor each chosen column \\(C_i\\) delete the vertical line \\(L_i\\) and insert the\nsunny line  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\; y-(n+1-i)=\\frac12\\bigl(x-i\\bigr).\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\tfrac12\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore \\(S_i\\) is\nsunny.  Because the line has a non‑vertical direction, the only lattice point\nof \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lying on it is its defining point \\((i,n+1-i)\\).  Consequently\n\\(S_i\\) covers exactly the unique point of the replaced column.\n\nAfter performing the replacement for the selected \\(k\\) columns we have:\n\n* exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (\\(S_i\\));\n* the remaining \\(n-k\\) vertical lines still cover all points of the untouched\n  columns;\n* the total number of lines remains \\(n\\).\n\nThus every integer \\(k\\) that satisfies (8) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nThe upper‑bound argument shows that no configuration can have more than the\nnumbers of sunny lines listed in (8).  The explicit construction shows that each\nvalue allowed by (8) is attainable.  Therefore the set of possible numbers of\nsunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1\\}, & n=3\\text{ or }n=4,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n∎"
